Server-side code reads the current session through `loopar.session`:

```javascript
import loopar from "loopar";

const userId  = loopar.session.user;       // current user id
const site    = loopar.session.site;       // current tenant
const isAdmin = loopar.session.is_admin;   // administrator flag

if (!loopar.session.user) {
  loopar.throw("Authentication required", 401);
}
```

## Looking Up Users

```javascript
// Fetch a user by id or email
const user = await loopar.getUser("user@example.com");

// Disable a user account
await loopar.disabledUser(userId);
```

## Login & Logout

Authentication is handled by the framework's `Auth` layer. A successful `login()` establishes a session and sets a CSRF cookie; `logout()` kills the session. You normally don't call these directly — the built-in auth pages and API routes do."}}]}, {"element": "div", "data": {"label": "Roles & Permissions", "key": "auth_permissions", "id": "auth_permissions", "name": "auth_permissions"}, "elements": [{"element": "title", "data": {"key": "title_auth_permissions", "text": "Roles & Permissions", "size": "md", "id": "title_auth_permissions", "name": "title_auth_permissions", "label": "Title auth_permissions"}}, {"element": "markdown", "data": {"key": "md_auth_permissions", "value": "Loopar's authorization model is **role-based with granular, per-document permissions**.

## Levels of Control

| Level | Example |
|-------|---------|
| **Document** | Who can read / write / delete `Customer` records |
| **Field** | Hide `salary` from non-HR roles |
| **Action** | Who can call `actionApprove` on an `Invoice` |

## Checking Permissions

`PermissionManager` resolves whether a user may perform an action:

```javascript
import { PermissionManager } from "loopar";

// Can this user delete Customer records?
const allowed = PermissionManager.can("Customer", "delete", username);

// All resolved permissions for a user
const perms = PermissionManager.getPermissions(username);
```

When permissions change, `PermissionManager.invalidate(username)` clears the cached set for that user."}}]}, {"element": "div", "data": {"label": "Controller Actions & Access", "key": "auth_actions", "id": "auth_actions", "name": "auth_actions"}, "elements": [{"element": "title", "data": {"key": "title_auth_actions", "text": "Controller Actions & Access", "size": "md", "id": "title_auth_actions", "name": "title_auth_actions", "label": "Title auth_actions"}}, {"element": "markdown", "data": {"key": "md_auth_actions", "value": "Only methods prefixed with `action` on a controller are reachable over HTTP. Everything else stays private to the server.

```javascript
import { BaseController } from "loopar";

export default class InvoiceController extends BaseController {
  // Reachable: GET/POST /api/Invoice/approve
  async actionApprove() { /* ... */ }

  // NOT reachable over HTTP — internal helper only
  recalcTotals() { /* ... */ }
}
```

`ActionScanner` walks every app and registers these actions so `PermissionManager` can guard them. Unauthenticated actions are surfaced through `ActionScanner.getPublicActions()`.

## Bypassing Permission Checks

Trusted server-side code can skip the permission check on a save:

```javascript
await doc.save({ ignore_permissions: true });
```

Use this sparingly — only in code paths you fully control."}}]}, {"element": "div", "data": {"label": "CSRF & Realtime", "key": "auth_csrf", "id": "auth_csrf", "name": "auth_csrf"}, "elements": [{"element": "title", "data": {"key": "title_auth_csrf", "text": "CSRF & Realtime", "size": "md", "id": "title_auth_csrf", "name": "title_auth_csrf", "label": "Title auth_csrf"}}, {"element": "markdown", "data": {"key": "md_auth_csrf", "value": "## CSRF Protection

